foreign  a lot of people were very skeptical  about  how would women how would we be with  these units how would we perform are we  going to be a liability  and a lot of those concerns aren't  unwarranted it's the unknown  um  but also on principle you never know  until you give people a chance to try so  that's  that's what our first group is there to  do we were the first ones not everyone  is going to be able to be a combat  Soldier  but everyone  is entitled to a chance the cultural  support team was a program started by  the  um  Army Special Operations program and it  was birthed out of many years of our  time in Afghanistan where you have  Special Operation Forces like Special  Forces Army Ranger Navy Seals who are  working in Afghanistan going after high  value targets from Al Qaeda and the  Taliban  so they go on these raids and they're  there to find these high value targets  and also gather critical intelligence  but one thing that they were missing was  being able to engage with the women and  children on Target because of the Afghan  culture  the American forces were not allowed to  talk to the women and children there was  this Niche need where that only women  could fill  when I first went to Afghanistan and was  embedded with the 75th Ranger regiment  for me  I I had uh some folks that understood  hey it has to be kind of lonely and  Uncharted being the only woman there  um and and some some Rangers that  befriended me which I appreciated  but initially a lot really kept their  distance it was about two weeks before  anyone really talked to me it was very  lonely it was uh not  not welcoming kind of a more cold  reception it was a lot of I think wait  and see and prove yourself  which  happens to even new Rangers so a lot of  it wasn't necessarily based on being a  woman but yes some of it was there was a  lot more I think scrutiny and skepticism  on how are we going to perform but it  wasn't an easy Road it definitely took a  while to change some hearts and Minds  within our own our own Special Operation  Forces  when I left  there was such an attitude shift when I  left these Rangers were saying you know  it's kind of weird that they don't even  let women go to go to Ranger school like  they let men who aren't in combat arms  go like why why can't women and when I  left one of the Rangers gave me their  their patch and said that'd be really  cool if you like  um  come a ranger one day  the reasons why Afghan women  were recruited to end up doing the  cultural support team job they were they  were dubbed the female tactical platoon  so that was the name for the Afghan  women we needed to build up the internal  Afghan capacity to be able to  govern and secure themselves  so this also included units that are  capable of doing these type of direct  action raids going after high value  targets  the roles were essentially the same  what is different though is  the level of Courage  that I think it takes to actually raise  your hand and do that job there's just  so much more risk for them agreeing to  associate and do this job do a job that  is Leaps and Bounds ahead of what Afghan  women are doing in society let alone the  military and let alone the highest  echelons of their military  they completely threaten what the  Taliban stands for and wants to  Institute under their governance  there absolutely was impact and there  was impact in  the advancement of women  the success of the cultural support team  program was huge in that  a lot of what we proved that we could do  there played a big role in the  Department of Defense ultimately  rescinding the women on combat ban now  the same goes for these Afghan women and  them proving themselves on the  battlefield right there with their male  Afghan counterparts culturally has huge  impacts on how women are viewed and  revered in society really I want these  stories to go to the next generation of  service women and hopefully they can be  inspired by it

